---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

const props = {
	title: "Makhtotah Platform",
	tagline:
		"AI-powered search engine for Arabic historical documents. The applied half of the Makhtotah work.",
	description:
		"OCR-backed search and discovery layer over Arabic manuscript collections.",
	status: "ongoing" as const,
	startDate: "Dec 2024",
	type: "product" as const,
	role: "Solo developer",
	team: "AthkaU Competition entry",
	stack: ["Flask", "Azure Vision", "Python", "MongoDB"],
	relatedProjects: [
		{ slug: "makhtotah-research", title: "Makhtotah Research" },
		{ slug: "arabic-ocr-hwr", title: "Arabic OCR/HWR with Qwen2" },
	],
	toc: [
		{ id: "what-it-is", label: "What it is" },
		{ id: "what-it-does", label: "What it does" },
		{ id: "stack", label: "Why this stack" },
		{ id: "where-its-going", label: "Where it's going" },
	],
};
---

<ProjectLayout {...props}>
	<h2 id="what-it-is">What it is</h2>
	<p>
		The product side of the Makhtotah work. Where the
		<a href="/projects/makhtotah-research">research project</a>
		is the model and methodology, the platform is the place an actual
		researcher, librarian, or curious reader lands when they want to
		<em>search</em> Arabic historical documents, not just OCR a single
		page.
	</p>
	<p>
		It started as an AthkaU Competition entry. The brief was simple: take
		the OCR work and put it behind something a human can use.
	</p>

	<h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Upload or browse digitized manuscript pages.</li>
		<li>OCR each page (Azure Vision in v1, with the custom pipeline coming in).</li>
		<li>Index the recognized text with manuscript metadata (era, condition, calligraphic style).</li>
		<li>Full-text search across the corpus, returning page-level matches with the original image alongside.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="stack">Why this stack</h2>
	<p>
		Flask because it stays out of the way; Azure Vision because it gave a
		usable v1 OCR baseline before the custom Makhtotah model was ready;
		MongoDB because manuscript metadata is irregular by nature and a
		document store maps cleanly to that. None of these are forever
		decisions: the architecture is built to swap the OCR engine without
		touching the search and UI layers.
	</p>

	<h2 id="where-its-going">Where it's going</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Replace Azure Vision with the custom OCR model from Makhtotah Research, on the historical-manuscript slice where commercial APIs lose.</li>
		<li>Add calligraphic-style filtering to search.</li>
		<li>Surface confidence per region, so researchers can flag low-confidence transcriptions instead of trusting them blindly.</li>
	</ul>
</ProjectLayout>
